<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.ascentcapital.eod</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>/Users/scott/IdeaProjects/ascent-capital/scripts/run_eod.sh</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      StartInterval, NOT StartCalendarInterval: same root-cause fix as
      com.ascentcapital.heartbeat.plist. StartCalendarInterval jobs are
      skipped entirely (no catch-up) if the Mac is asleep at the exact
      scheduled wall-clock moment — that silent skip is what turned a sleeping
      laptop into 2 missed scheduled rebalances and a 19-day outage. This job
      is the one that actually trades, so it needs the fix more than the
      heartbeat does, not less.

      Original intent (still true, now enforced in Python instead of launchd):
      09:00 local (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh, UTC+7) Tue..Sat, which maps to the
      Mon..Fri US sessions after Alpaca's ~17:00 PT settlement (see the prior
      version of this file / git history for the full zoneinfo mapping and
      the settlement-margin reasoning). None of that logic needs to live in
      the plist: run_eod.sh gates on the US market date via
      ascent/utils/market_time.py + heartbeat_check.py's holiday calendar and
      exits immediately on a non-trading day, and run_all_agents.py's
      same-session guard (`already_ran_for_session(today)`, grep it) exits
      immediately if today's session already has a logged run, unless
      --force/--date is passed. Neither is touched by this change. That
      combination is what makes firing this job more than once a day safe.

      Interval choice: tighter than the heartbeat's 21600s (6h) on purpose.
      The heartbeat is a passive health check where a multi-hour catch-up
      delay is fine; this job is the one whose late catch-up cost real
      Track-B settlement margin and 2 missed rebalances. 3600s (1h) means a
      laptop that wakes at any point in the day is at most ~1h from a
      catch-up attempt, comfortably inside the 1-2h settlement margin the
      original 09:00-vs-08:00 choice was built around, while every
      redundant firing inside the same trading session or on a closed day
      is a cheap no-op via the guards above.
    -->
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>3600</integer>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/scott/IdeaProjects/ascent-capital/logs/launchd_stdout.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/scott/IdeaProjects/ascent-capital/logs/launchd_stderr.log</string>

    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
